{
  "schema": 1,
  "scope": "Two diagnostic followups using the unchanged frozen contract_throughput fixture; 15 rotated rounds of three variants in each followup. Rust1.100.0, CPU4, whole-process perf instructions/cycles retained.",
  "edge_experiment": "Old source with the patched dependency stack versus that same checkout with only the unused domain->core manifest edge and corresponding lockfile edge removed, plus current source. No Rust source change in the isolated old variant. Canonical implementation and fixture hashes are identical in all three. Edge removal alone increases the measured median by about 3.9%, explaining only part of the current 15.6% difference in this diagnostic.",
  "layout_experiment": "Current default binary versus two explicit .text virtual addresses, 0x19340 and 0x19360. Both explicit-link .text sections are byte-for-byte identical (279510 bytes) and their addresses differ by 32. The canonical routine moves from 0x1f220 to 0x1f240 with the same 0x2a6 size. ELF sections/segments, symbol tables, raw .text and binary hashes are retained. Equal-length copied executable names control argv path length for these runs.",
  "interpretation": [
    "The two explicit-link controls have median encoding-loop rates 358.35363 and 310.23570 ns/op. The default current layout gives 360.03428 ns/op. Identical instruction bytes with changed mapped placement reproduce a timing effect of the same magnitude as the legacy review flag.",
    "This establishes link-layout sensitivity, including code and associated data placement. It does not isolate instruction cache alignment, data-cache effects, front-end stalls or one particular function as the exact microarchitectural cause. Explicit section placement changes ELF section ordering relative to the default; the explicit pair is the controlled comparison.",
    "All observations are process-loop averages over100000 encodings; reported medians are medians of those averages, not individual-call p50. Perf counters cover encoding, append, setup and reporting. All rounds/outliers are retained; no resampling/equivalence claim.",
    "The linker flags exist only in these diagnostic commands. No production configuration, padding, function alignment workaround, serializer source, benchmark fixture, dependency security policy or validation changed.",
    "The original 31-pair performance flag remains recorded and unwaived. Layout sensitivity is a diagnosis, not automatic acceptance or proof that all consumer binaries are unaffected. Candidate qualification and performance conclusions remain open."
  ],
